WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.660
Take a mouse with no working rods and no working cones.

2
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.940
Can it still tell day from night?

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.340
Normally, rods and cones catch the light.

4
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.240
Bipolar cells pass their signals on to ganglion cells.

5
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:20.223
Ganglion cells are the retina's output neurons.

6
00:00:21.163 --> 00:00:23.283
Their axons carry its signals to the brain.

7
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:28.983
In 1999, two papers studied mice that lack both rods and cones.

8
00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:31.703
How do you see a clock in a mouse?

9
00:00:32.643 --> 00:00:33.643
Watch its running wheel.

10
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:37.273
A mouse is nocturnal, so it runs in the dark.

11
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:39.753
Each line of the record is one day.

12
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:46.433
Stack the days. If running starts at the same time each day, near lights-off, the

13
00:00:46.433 --> 00:00:47.993
clock looks locked to the light cycle.

14
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:53.577
In these rodless, coneless mice, light still shifted the timing of their daily

15
00:00:53.577 --> 00:00:54.577
running.

16
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.900
Light still suppressed melatonin from their pineal gland.

17
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.980
Work on the pupil followed two years later.

18
00:01:01.983 --> 00:01:06.103
So something in the eye, other than rods and cones, was still measuring light.

19
00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:10.357
Responses like these use light without forming an image.

20
00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:12.397
They are called non-image-forming.

21
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:14.887
Two are best established.

22
00:01:15.827 --> 00:01:17.507
The first is setting the body clock.

23
00:01:18.327 --> 00:01:19.927
The second is narrowing the pupil.

24
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.240
The brain's master clock is the suprachiasmatic nucleus, or SCN.

25
00:01:26.183 --> 00:01:29.023
Light reaching it keeps the clock aligned with day and night.

26
00:01:29.023 --> 00:01:31.403
That is called photoentrainment.

27
00:01:32.233 --> 00:01:36.133
The pupil reflex is driven from the olivary pretectal nucleus.

28
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:38.863
Which cells were measuring the light?

29
00:01:39.723 --> 00:01:43.523
In these mice, the answer was a small set of retinal ganglion cells.

30
00:01:44.583 --> 00:01:48.643
They are called intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells.

31
00:01:49.503 --> 00:01:51.063
For short, ipRGCs.

32
00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:56.103
Each makes its own light response with a pigment called melanopsin.

33
00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:59.183
It also relays signals from rods and cones.

34
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:04.647
In mice, these cells are the main route by which light reaches the clock and the

35
00:02:04.647 --> 00:02:05.647
pupil.

36
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.630
Three questions come next.

37
00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.470
What does the cells' own light response look like?

38
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.860
How does light become a voltage inside them?

39
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.480
And how do we know the clock and pupil depend on them?

40
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.050
Hold one picture in mind.

41
00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.070
Two light signals arrive at one cell, and leave by one exit.
